
  - [1mKey-pattern redaction (denylist)[0m — the logger walks structured fields and replaces values whose keys match [38;5;153mpassword[39m,
    [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, etc. with [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Low friction, catches the known cases. [1mFails open[0m: a field nobody
    anticipated ([38;5;153mpwd2[39m, [38;5;153manswer[39m) logs in the clear.
  - [1mAllowlist[0m — only explicitly-declared fields are serialized; everything else is dropped. [1mFails closed[0m, which is the
    right direction for auth. Costs friction at every new log site, and in my experience under-specified allowlists get
    worked around with string interpolation, which bypasses redaction entirely.
  - [1mNo redaction, call-site discipline[0m — cheapest, and it is exactly how credentials end up in log aggregators. I don't
    recommend it for an app whose only flow is a login form.

  Worth separating from all three: [1musernames[0m. A username is PII but is also the single most useful field for correlating
  a production report to a log line. Redacting it makes logs safer and substantially less useful; the usual middle
  ground is logging a stable hash or a truncated form.

  Once a sink exists, redaction is not cheap to add retroactively — anything already shipped is already shipped. That
  argues for deciding it now, which is why I'm asking before the design rather than after.

  [1mMy recommendation: key-pattern redaction as a backstop, plus a rule in the design that credential-bearing objects are
[0m  [1mnever passed to the logger at all[0m — log [38;5;153m{ hasPassword: true }[39m, never the value. The pattern match catches mistakes;
  the rule prevents them. For usernames I'd log them in full in Node and hashed in the browser sink, but I'd rather you
  pick.
